{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query=\"Intervals.icu October 2026 workout_doc export heart rate range schema Garmin\" numResults=5'","aggregated_output":"Title: API access to Intervals.icu\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-access-to-intervals-icu/609?page=20\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nYes, Intervals.icu translates fixed power (and other) targets into a range. For power this is only done for outdoor workouts. The default range for power and pace is 2.5%. For HR it is 1.5%. If the athlete has changed Garmin stuff in /settings then these are the defaults:\n\nYou can also supply powerRange, hrRange and paceRange query parameters to the call (e.g. `powerRange=5.0`) for 5%. Use 0.0 to disable range. I am going to update the docs.\n\nDevices beep and so on if you aren’t in the range and this is impossible outdoors and for HR and pace if there isn’t some spread.\n\nI have added these fields to workout_doc and will deploy Thurs AM (GMT+2):\n\n```\npublic Integer lthr;\npublic Float threshold_pace;\npublic String pace_units;\n\n---\n\nTitle: Downloading planned workouts from the API\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/downloading-planned-workouts-from-the-api/93737\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nYou can download planned workouts from an athlete’s calendar in zwo (Zwift), fit, mrc and erg format (CALENDAR:READ scope required):\n\n```auto\n$ curl 'https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/0/events?category=WORKOUT&ext=zwo&resolve=true' \\\n    -H 'Authorization: Bearer d842c1fc25f241e5ae440d09756448a9'\n\n```\n\nThe optional category parameter specifies what sort of events to return (planned workouts in this case). The ext parameter the format (leave out if you don’t need the file). The resolve=true parameter converts power (and heart rate etc.) from units like % of FTP into watts. The default date range is a week from today (use oldest and newest parameters in ISO-8601 format to change).\n\nExample abridged response:\n\n```auto\n[\n  {\n    \"id\": 46696127,\n    \"start_date_local\": \"2024-11-21T07:00:00\",\n    \"type\": \"Ride\",\n    \"category\": \"WORKOUT\",\n    \"name\": \"Big Gear Grinds\",\n    \"description\": \"...\",\n    ...\n    \"workout_doc\": {\n      \"description\": \"A great session for building cycling specific strength both in and out of the saddle.\",\n      \"target\": \"POWER\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        {\n          \"text\": \"Welcome to the ...\",\n          \"duration\": 180,\n          \"warmup\": true,\n          \"ramp\": true,\n          \"power\": { \"start\": 35.0, \"end\": 55.0, \"units\": \"%ftp\" },\n          \"cadence\": { \"value\": 85.0, \"units\": \"rpm\" },\n          \"_power\": { \"value\": 126.0, \"start\": 98.0, \"end\": 154.0 }\n        },\n      ],\n      ...\n    },\n    ...\n    \"workout_filename\": \"Big_Gear_Grinds.zwo\",\n    \"workout_file_base64\": \"PD94bWwgdmVy...\"\n  }\n]\n\n```\n\nThe workout_file_base64 field contains the file in the specified format (zwo in this case) encoded as base 64 text.\n\nThe workout_doc field contains the workout in native Intervals.icu format. If you don’t already have a parser for one of the other formats then this might be easier to work with.\n\nThis is how the workout_doc field is mapped in Intervals.icu:\n\n```java\npublic class WorkoutDoc {\n\n    public String description;\n    public Map<String, String> description_locale;\n    public Integer duration;\n    public Float distance;\n    public Integer ftp;\n    public Integer lthr;\n    public Float threshold_pace; // meters/sec\n    public String pace_units; // SECS_100M, SECS_100Y, MINS_KM, MINS_MILE, SECS_500M\n    public SportSettings sportSettings;\n    public String category;\n    public WorkoutTarget target;\n    public List<Step> steps;\n    public List<Object> zoneTimes; // sometimes array of ints otherwise array of objects\n    public Map<String, String> options;\n    public List<String> locales;\n\n    public enum Option {\n        category, pool_length, power\n    }\n\n    public static class Step {\n        public String text;\n        public Map<String, String> text_locale;\n        public Integer duration;\n        public Float distance;\n        public Boolean until_lap_press;\n        public Integer reps;\n        public Boolean warmup;\n        public Boolean cooldown;\n        public String intensity; // \"active\", \"rest\", \"warmup\", \"cooldown\", \"recovery\", \"interval\", \"other\"\n        public List<Step> steps;\n        public Boolean ramp;\n        public Boolean freeride;\n        public Boolean maxeffort;\n        public Value power;\n        public Value hr;\n        public Value pace;\n        public Value cadence;\n        public Boolean hidepower;\n\n        // these are filled in with actual watts, bpm etc. when resolve=true parameter is supplied to the endpoint\n        public Value _power;\n        public Value _hr;\n        public Value _pace;\n        public Float _distance;\n    }\n\n    public static class Value {\n        public Double value;\n        public Double start;\n        public Double end;\n        public String units;\n        public String target;\n        public Integer mmp_duration;\n    }\n}\n\n/** How should workouts be performed? Target power, HR or pace? */\npublic enum WorkoutTarget {\n    AUTO(\"A\"), POWER(\"W\"), HR(\"H\"), PACE(\"P\");\n}\n\n```\n\n---\n\nTitle: How to send structured workouts with repeats and pace/power ranges via API?\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/how-to-send-structured-workouts-with-repeats-and-pace-power-ranges-via-api/130971\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n[{“id”:128751902,“start_date_local”:“2026-08-17T00:00:00”,“icu_training_load”:null,“icu_atl”:null,“icu_ctl”:null,“type”:“Run”,“carbs_used”:null,“ss_p_max”:null,“ss_w_prime”:null,“ss_cp”:null,“calendar_id”:1,“uid”:“01263f45-93f2-42bf-bee2-872fdcede371”,“athlete_id”:“i611360”,“category”:“WORKOUT”,“end_date_local”:“2026-08-18T00:00:00”,“name”:“TEST 2x1km Pace Range V2”,“description”:“- 10m 5:30-6:16/km Pace\\n\\n2x\\n- 1km 3:30-3:50/km Pace\\n- 2m 5:10-6:00/km Pace\\n\\n- 10m 5:30-6:16/km\n\n...\n\nThe `workout_doc` looks correct. However, when I open the workout on my Garmin, I only see the duration. The pace range is missing. Is the `pace` field in `workout_doc` supposed to be converted to a pace target when the workout is pushed to Garmin? The same problem when upload a FIT file.\n\n...\n\nThat’s intentional. Both Pace and Power targets are sent as a % of threshold. This makes workouts portable over longer time (where threshold changes) and in between athletes with different thresholds.\nSo you need threshold and zones to be configured.\n\n...\n\nYou can use absolute pace and power values but for the reasons above, it is discouraged.\nYou need to set threshold and zones in Intervals to get correct load and full analysis. No way around that.\n\nI don’t know exactly how absolute Pace is handled but for absolute Power (on Garmin - not every brand), you have to send a value over 1000. Any value below 1000 is interpreted as a percentage of threshold. For values above 1000, the first digit (the 1) is dropped and the rest is used as absolute power target value.\n\n- 50 = 50%\n- 100 = 100%\n- 400 = 400%\n- 1050 = 50W\n- 1100 = 100W\n- 11100 = 1100W\n\nBut without threshold and zones, Intervals can’t attribute a Load to a workout with absolute values, so it won’t work.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Events created via API with `workout_doc`: the web download produces an empty FIT while the API download does not — which representation feeds device sync? - Questions & Help - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/events-created-via-api-with-workout-doc-the-web-download-produces-an-empty-fit-while-the-api-download-does-not-which-representation-feeds-device-sync/132326\nPublished: 2026-09-08T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Tomas_Povina, R2Tom, MedTechCD\nHighlights:\n## 1. The same event exports differently through the web and through the API\n\nOne of our pushed events (`Ritmo Suave`, a single 10 km step):\n\n| download path | file size | `workout_step` messages | `num_valid_steps` |\n\n|—|—|—|—|\n\n| API, `GET /events?category=WORKOUT&ext=fit` | **119 bytes** | **1** | **1** |\n\n| Web UI download button | **92 bytes** | **0** | **0** |\n\nSame event, same day, no edits in between. The web download was repeated after a full re-upsert\n\nof every event, so this isn’t a stale-cache effect. `GET` on the event returns\n\n`workout_doc.steps` intact either way.\n\n## 2. A controlled A/B pair isolates the cause to the *vehicle*\n\nTwo events describing the **same** workout (10 km @ 5:30/km), differing only in how it was sent:\n\n| control | payload sent | `workout_doc` after GET | FIT via **API** | FIT via **web** |\n\n|—|—|—|—|—|\n\n| **A** | `description: “- 10km 5:30/km Pace”` only | 1 step (compiled by Intervals) | 121 b / **1 step** | 121 b / **1 step** |\n\n| **B** | `workout_doc` only, no description | 1 step (exactly as sent) | 119 b / **1 step** | 92 b / **0 steps** |\n\nA workout authored as **text** exports correctly through both paths. One authored as\n\n**`workout_doc`** exports correctly only through the API — the web generator appears not to see\n\nsteps that were uploaded as `workout_doc` rather than compiled from the description text.\n\nThis lines up with [#124215]( API: Create or update workout without using description text syntax? ),\n\nwhere a user created a workout via `workout_doc`, got well-formed steps back from the backend, and\n\nstill found “the pace range is missing” once it reached Garmin — an independent report on a real\n\ndevice rather than on a download.\n\n## 3. Pace targets need the athlete’s threshold pace — solved, but worth documenting\n\nInitially the exported step came out with no target at all:\n\n```\n\nthreshold\\_pace: null → 92 bytes step 0: duration = DISTANCE 1000000 (10 km, cm) target = OPEN\n\n```\n\nThe pace was present in the compiled `workout_doc` the whole time —\n\n`{“pace”:{“units”:“secs/km”,“value”:330},“distance”:10000,“duration”:3300}`, and Intervals even\n\nderived the 3300 s duration from it — but it never reached the FIT.\n\nFollowing [#130706]( Pace targets lost in Garmin export for API-created running workouts — steps arrive on watch as \"No Target\" (parsed correctly in workout_doc) ),\n\nwe set a Run threshold pace in the athlete’s sport settings and re-exported the same event:\n\n```\n\nthreshold\\_pace set → 146 bytes step 0: duration = DISTANCE 1000000 target = SPEED 2.955–3.106 m/s\n\n```\n\nThat’s a 5:22–5:38 /km band around the 5:30 we prescribed, so a single prescribed value is widened\n\nby roughly ±8 s/km on export — good to know, and it confirmed for us that we should keep emitting\n\nsingle values rather than bands.\n\nSo this one is a **configuration prerequisite** , not a bug. It’s just an invisible one from an\n\nintegrator’s side: an unset threshold silently strips targets from otherwise valid workouts, and\n\nnothing in the API response hints at it. A line in the upload guide would save the next person the\n\nsame hunt.\n\n...\n\nWe followed your advice and switched our emitter to the description field:\nsame workout that exported as 92 bytes / 0 steps now comes out as 450 bytes\nwith all 8 steps and proper speed targets. The preflight idea (checking the\nathlete’s zones before syncing) is implemented too.\n\n---\n\nTitle: API access to Intervals.icu\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-access-to-intervals-icu/609\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nUpdate 9th October 2026: Garmin terms and conditions have been updated and It is likely that we will have to block access to Garmin data via the API soon.\n\nUpdate 14th October 2026: The revisions to the agreement are part of the planned reopening of the Garmin Connect Developer Program. There will be an easier path for individuals and apps to access data from Garmin devices.\n\n...\n\n- The workout description is now parsed. This means that workouts created or updated via the API have training load calculated and get “time in zones” and so on.\n\n...\n\n`POST /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events`\n\n```auto\n{\n  \"category\": \"WORKOUT\",\n  \"start_date_local\": \"2021-04-29T00:00:00\",\n  \"type\": \"Ride\",\n  \"filename\": \"4x8m.zwo\",\n  \"file_contents\": \"<?xml version=\\\"1.0\\\" encoding=\\\"UTF-8\\\" standalone=\\\"yes\\\"?>\\n<workout_file>...\"\n\n...\n\nResponse:\n\n```auto\n{\n  \"id\": 610062,\n  \"icu_training_load\": 116,\n  ...\n  \"name\": \"4x8m VO2 Max\",\n  \"description\": \"These are horribly nasty.\\nCategory: Horrible\\n\\n- 20m 60% 90-100rpm\\n\\nMain set 4x\\n- 8m 110%\\n- 8m 50%\\n\\n- 10m 60%\\n\",\n  ...\n  \"workout_doc\": {\n    \"steps\": [\n      {\n        \"power\": {\"units\": \"%ftp\", \"value\": 60},\n        \"cadence\": {\"end\": 100, \"start\": 90, \"units\": \"rpm\"}, \"duration\": 1200\n      }, ...\n    ],\n    \"duration\": 5640,\n    \"zoneTimes\": [1920, 1800, 0, 0, 1920, 0, 0, 0],\n    \"hrZoneTimes\": [1920, 1800, 0, 0, 1920, 0, 0, 0]\n  },\n  \"icu_intensity\": 86.04798\n}\n\n```\n\n...\n\nThere is a catch: Workouts have a “workout_doc” field which is the parsed workout steps. Currently the parser is only available client side. So before you can download a workout in a different format or have it sync to Garmin Connect you need to edit it using the Intervals.icu app so the workout_doc field is populated.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
